    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d443">

                <pb facs="0540" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg445"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13811: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-12-22T11:00:00+08:00">December 22, 1936—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 22—2:26 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>371. T. V. Soong accompanied by Donald returned to Nanking by air yesterday. It
                    is believed that T. V. is returning to Sian today possibly accompanied by Madame
                    Chiang Kai-shek.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d443-f1"/> Neither Donald <pb facs="0541"
                        n="446" xml:id="pg446"/> nor T. V. brings very good news from Sian. Donald
                    is reported to have said that real power at Sian is Yang Hu Cheng; that question
                    of Chiang’s release might be settled except for the uncertainty of Yang’s
                    attitude and those under him.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, code texts to Peiping and Tokyo by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d443-f1" n="19" xml:id="d443-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Soong, Mme. Chiang, and W. H. Donald left by airplane for Sian that day.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
